    howItWorks:
      "Keflavík is a town on the Reykjanes peninsula in south-western Iceland; in the mid-1990s it merged with Njarðvík and Hafnir to form the municipality of Reykjanesbær, the combined urban settlement of Keflavík and Njarðvík holding about 22,000 people in 2025 and the whole municipality about 25,000 in mid-2026; Iceland has only the state and municipalities, the municipality running schools, planning and local services while the state runs health care and police. The name means driftwood bay, and the town grew from the sixteenth century on fishing and fish processing amid a country of basalt rubble and moss. During the Second World War the American military built the airport beside the town, and through the Cold War Naval Air Station Keflavik was an important base for watching the northern seas, the American forces withdrawing in 2006 and the base passing to civil and residential use. Keflavík International Airport lies at the town's edge as Iceland's international gateway, where nearly every flight in and out of the country lands, with Reykjavík about 50 km north-east. Many musicians came from here in the 1960s and 1970s, earning it the nickname of the Beatles town. Newcomers most often go wrong by treating the airport as part of Reykjavík, when it stands in another municipality nearly an hour from the city.",
